equality \\(-13(r+5) + 25 > 4(r-10)\\) for \\(r\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Distribute the constants inside the parentheses.\n2. Combine like terms.\n3. Isolate the variable \\(r\\).\n4. Express the solution in interval notation.\n\nLet's start with the given inequality:\n\\[\n-13(r+5) + 25 > 4(r-10)\n\\]\n\nFirst, distribute \\(-13\\) and \\(4\\) inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n-13r - 65 + 25 > 4r - 40\n\\]\n\nNext, combine the constant terms on the left side:\n\\[\n-13r - 40 > 4r - 40\n\\]\n\nNow, add \\(13r\\) to both sides to get all terms involving \\(r\\) on one side:\n\\[\n-40 > 17r - 40\n\\]\n\nAdd \\(40\\) to both sides to isolate the term with \\(r\\):\n\\[\n0 > 17r\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n0 > 17r \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 17r < 0\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by \\(17\\) (since \\(17\\) is positive, the inequality direction does not change):\n\\[\nr < 0\n\\]\n\nExpress the solution in interval notation:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, 0)}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(-\\infty, 0)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:169"}
